<section class="section actone" id="threat" aria-labelledby="actone-title">
  <div class="container">
    <header class="actone__head reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow">Why your last upgrade didn't pay off</p>
      <h2 class="actone__title" id="actone-title">
        The number that should bother you.
      </h2>
      <p class="actone__stat ph" tabindex="0">~30%</p>
      <p class="actone__stat-note">
        According to the EPA, the average commercial building wastes about a
        third of the energy it consumes. Most hotels have never had anyone
        qualified find out where.
      </p>
    </header>

    <div class="actone__body">
      <article class="block reveal">
        <h3 class="block__title">Every fix you bought was sold in isolation</h3>
        <p>
          You added lighting. Maybe controls, maybe solar. Each vendor optimized
          the one thing they sell and left. But a building isn't a pile of
          separate upgrades. It's one electrical system, and when pieces are
          bolted on without anyone designing how they work together, they
          interfere. Power quality degrades. Equipment runs harder than it
          should and fails sooner. The savings on the brochure quietly leak away
          in distortion nobody is accountable for.
        </p>
        <p>
          That interference has a literal signature in your panels, and it is
          measurable. It is also invisible on a utility bill, which is exactly
          why it survives for years.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="block reveal">
        <h3 class="block__title">Nobody was responsible for the whole building</h3>
        <p>
          There is a reason it ends up incoherent. Every vendor in this industry
          gets paid to install their box, so every one of them optimizes their
          own piece and none of them owns the result. The system was never
          incoherent by accident. It was incoherent by incentive.
        </p>
        <p class="block__turn">
          We get paid to do the opposite: to find where your building loses
          money and design the stack that makes it work as one system. The
          verification is the product. The equipment is just whatever the
          verification points to.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .actone {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--line);
  }
  .actone__head {
    max-width: 60rem;
  }
  .actone__title {
    margin-top: var(--space-xs);
    font-size: var(--step-3);
    max-width: 16ch;
  }
  .actone__stat {
    display: inline-block;
    margin-top: var(--space-l);
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: clamp(3.5rem, 9vw, 6rem);
    line-height: 0.9;
    color: var(--gold-text);
    letter-spacing: -0.04em;
  }
  .actone__stat-note {
    margin-top: var(--space-s);
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--body);
    max-width: 52ch;
  }

  .actone__body {
    margin-top: var(--space-2xl);
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 320px), 1fr));
    gap: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 4rem);
  }
  .block__title {
    font-size: var(--step-2);
    max-width: 20ch;
    margin-bottom: var(--space-m);
  }
  .block p {
    color: var(--body);
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    line-height: 1.65;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
  .block p + p {
    margin-top: var(--space-s);
  }
  .block__turn {
    color: var(--ink) !important;
    font-family: var(--font-serif);
    font-size: var(--step-1) !important;
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: 1.5 !important;
    letter-spacing: -0.01em;
    margin-top: var(--space-m) !important;
  }

  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
    .actone__body .block:nth-child(2).reveal {
      transition-delay: 0.1s;
    }
  }
</style>
